    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d188">

                <pb facs="0990" n="963" sameAs="frus:frus1989-92v31/pg963"/>

                <head>188. Telegram From the Delegation to the Nuclear and Space Talks in Geneva to
                    the Department of State<anchor n="1" xml:id="d188-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">00241.</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Geneva</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1991-01-10T17:57:00+00:00">January 10, 1991,
                            1757Z</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>START: Soviet Response to Baker Letter</item>
                </list>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>This is Blackbird—031. Secret—Entire Text.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Late on Thursday, January 10, Nazarkin called me from Moscow. He said he
                        would return to Geneva Saturday night and that his return had been delayed
                        by working on the Soviet response to Secretary Baker’s letter of January
                            4.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d188-f2"/></item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Nazarkin said that Shevardnadze’s response was not yet completed and he
                        was not absolutely certain when it would be delivered. Based on decisions
                        made thus far, however, Nazarkin said it <pb facs="0991" n="964"
                            xml:id="pg964"/> was “safe to say” that the United States would be
                        “fully satisfied” with the response. In addition, Nazarkin said, he had
                        spent time working on issues not included in the Houston package and had
                        been successful in getting approval to offer appropriate compromises.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>Nazarkin said that he hoped that the Shevardnadze response would clear the
                        way for high level meetings (i.e., Bartholomew-Obukhov) in Geneva next
                        week.</item>
                    <label>5.</label>
                    <item>I will meet Nazarkin early Monday<anchor n="3" xml:id="d188-f3"/> and
                        should have a readout for Washington at open of business Washington time
                        Monday morning.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Burt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
